# Run lock: no reentrant acquisition, owner-safe release

## What to build

Jira KAN-74 / GE-13. `acquireLock(runDir)` in `src/state/state.ts:180–200` is reentrant inside one process and its release is unsafe, which bites anyone embedding the API. Repro: `const r1 = await acquireLock(dir); const r2 = await acquireLock(dir);` both succeed (the second one finds a lock containing its own PID, treats it as stale, deletes it and re-creates it); then `await r1()` deletes the lock that `r2` believes it holds, so a third caller can take the "held" lock. Calling a release twice also deletes whatever lock is there by then. Fix it in `src/state/state.ts` only:

1. A second acquisition of the same lock file while this process still holds it is **denied** with the existing `LockError` (defined in `src/state/state.ts:162` — `src/state/errors.ts` has no lock error; do not add a new class). This also holds for two concurrent `acquireLock` calls: exactly one wins.
2. Each acquisition gets a random per-acquisition token, written into the lock file as a second line (`<pid>\n<token>\n`) and remembered in a module-level registry. Its release function only unlinks the file if the registry and the file still show that token; a second call is a no-op.
3. Readers that parse the lock file (`lockHolder`, and the stale check inside `acquireLock`) read only the **first line** as the PID, so both the new format and old `<pid>\n` files (written by older versions and by existing tests) keep working.
4. A lock file that contains this process's PID but is **not** in the in-process registry is still treated as stale and taken over (it was left by an earlier process that had the same PID, which is common in containers). Dead PIDs are taken over as before; a live other PID still throws `run <id> is locked by the running process <pid>; wait for it or stop it`.

## Context: files to read before implementing

- `src/state/state.ts` (lines 1–5 imports; lines 160–200 `LOCK_FILE`, `LockError`, `isAlive`, `lockHolder`, `acquireLock`) — the code to change.
- `src/runner/run.ts` (lines 155–185 `run()`, lines 214–245 `resume()`) — callers: acquire at start, `finally { await release(); }`. Each takes the lock once per process; nothing re-enters, so they need no change.
- `src/suite/orchestrator.ts` (lines 150–190 suite start, lines 194–215 `resumeSuite`) — locks the suite folder (`acquireLock(suiteDir)`), a different file from the feature runs' locks; feature runs are separate child processes. Must keep working.
- `src/cleanup/scan.ts` (lines 91, 122) and `src/launch/preflight.ts` (lines 169–180 `assertNotRunning`) — call `lockHolder(dir)`; it must parse the new two-line format.
- `src/state/resume.test.ts` (lines 34–54) — CLI test: a lock file `${process.pid}\n` written by the test (a *different*, live process from the CLI's point of view) must still be refused with `is locked by the running process <pid>`, and a dead-PID lock taken over; after the run the lock file is gone.
- `src/runner/run-retry.test.ts` (lines 46–74) — in-process run: lock exists while waiting for a retry and is removed on quit.
- `src/cleanup/scan.test.ts` (lines 33–38) and `src/launch/preflight.test.ts` (lines 99–112) — write old-format `${pid}\n` lock files; must keep passing.
- `src/state/state.test.ts` (lines 1–13 setup, `dir` temp folder per test) — where the regression tests go.

## Patterns to follow

**Current code (`src/state/state.ts:173–200`):**
```typescript
/** The PID holding the run's lock, or undefined when there is no lock, it is stale, or this process holds it. */
export async function lockHolder(runDir: string): Promise<number | undefined> {
  const pid = Number((await readFile(path.join(runDir, LOCK_FILE), "utf8").catch(() => "")).trim());
  return Number.isInteger(pid) && pid > 0 && pid !== process.pid && isAlive(pid) ? pid : undefined;
}

export async function acquireLock(runDir: string): Promise<() => Promise<void>> {
  const file = path.join(runDir, LOCK_FILE);
  for (;;) {
    try {
      await writeFile(file, `${process.pid}\n`, { flag: "wx" });
      return async () => {
        await rm(file, { force: true });
      };
    } catch (err) {
      if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "EEXIST") throw err;
    }
    const pid = Number((await readFile(file, "utf8").catch(() => "")).trim());
    if (Number.isInteger(pid) && pid > 0 && pid !== process.pid && isAlive(pid)) {
      throw new LockError(`run ${path.basename(runDir)} is locked by the running process ${pid}; wait for it or stop it`);
    }
    await rm(file, { force: true });
  }
}
```

**Target shape (adapt, keep the doc comments up to date):**
```typescript
import { createHash, randomUUID } from "node:crypto";

/** Lock files held by this process (resolved path → token of the acquisition holding it). */
const heldLocks = new Map<string, string>();

/** The PID (first line) and token (second line) of a lock file's content. */
function parseLock(text: string): { pid: number; token: string | undefined } {
  const [first = "", second] = text.split("\n");
  return { pid: Number(first.trim()), token: second?.trim() || undefined };
}

export async function acquireLock(runDir: string): Promise<() => Promise<void>> {
  const file = path.resolve(runDir, LOCK_FILE);
  const name = path.basename(runDir);
  if (heldLocks.has(file)) throw new LockError(`run ${name} is already locked by this process; release it first`);
  const token = randomUUID();
  heldLocks.set(file, token); // reserved synchronously, before any await: a concurrent call is refused above
  try {
    for (;;) {
      try {
        await writeFile(file, `${process.pid}\n${token}\n`, { flag: "wx" });
        break;
      } catch (err) {
        if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "EEXIST") throw err;
      }
      const { pid } = parseLock(await readFile(file, "utf8").catch(() => ""));
      if (Number.isInteger(pid) && pid > 0 && pid !== process.pid && isAlive(pid)) {
        throw new LockError(`run ${name} is locked by the running process ${pid}; wait for it or stop it`);
      }
      await rm(file, { force: true }); // dead PID, or our PID left by an earlier process: stale
    }
  } catch (err) {
    heldLocks.delete(file);
    throw err;
  }
  return async () => {
    if (heldLocks.get(file) !== token) return; // released already
    heldLocks.delete(file);
    const { token: onDisk } = parseLock(await readFile(file, "utf8").catch(() => ""));
    if (onDisk === token) await rm(file, { force: true });
  };
}
```
`lockHolder` uses `parseLock(...).pid` instead of `Number(text.trim())`.

**Error handling:** reuse `LockError` (`export class LockError extends Error {}`); messages start with `run <basename(runDir)> ...` like the existing one.

**Test pattern (`src/state/state.test.ts`):**
```typescript
let dir: string;
beforeEach(async () => { dir = await mkdtemp(path.join(tmpdir(), "graph-execution-state-")); });
afterEach(async () => { await rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true, maxRetries: 10, retryDelay: 100 }); });
describe("StateStore", () => { it("saves atomically and loads back", async () => { ... }); });
```

## Step-by-step tasks

### UPDATE `src/state/state.ts`

- **IMPLEMENT**: the target shape above: `randomUUID` import added to the existing `node:crypto` import (line 1), the module-level `heldLocks` map, a private `parseLock` helper, the new `acquireLock` and release, and `lockHolder` reading the first line. Update the doc comment of `acquireLock` to say: a second acquisition while this process holds the lock throws `LockError`; the release removes the lock only while it is still this acquisition's, and is idempotent.
- **PATTERN**: the existing `acquireLock` loop (`src/state/state.ts:184–200`).
- **IMPORTS**: `import { createHash, randomUUID } from "node:crypto";` (`readFile`, `rm`, `writeFile` are already imported from `node:fs/promises`).
- **GOTCHA**: reserve the registry entry synchronously before the first `await`, or two concurrent `acquireLock(dir)` calls both pass the check, the loser sees its own PID in the file, treats it as stale and deletes the winner's lock. Remove the reservation on every failure path. Key the map by `path.resolve(...)` so `dir` and `dir/` are the same lock. Keep `LOCK_FILE`, `LockError`, `isAlive` and the `lockHolder` signature unchanged (other modules import them). `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on: destructured array items are `string | undefined`. Do not touch `run.ts`/`orchestrator.ts`: one acquire + `finally` release per process is correct.
- **VALIDATE**: `npx tsc --noEmit`

### UPDATE `src/state/state.test.ts` (regression tests)

- **IMPLEMENT**: add `describe("acquireLock", ...)` (and import `acquireLock`, `LockError`, `LOCK_FILE`, `lockHolder` from `./state.js`; `existsSync` from `node:fs`; `readFile`, `writeFile` from `node:fs/promises`):
  - `it("refuses a second acquisition in the same process until released")`: `const release = await acquireLock(dir); await expect(acquireLock(dir)).rejects.toBeInstanceOf(LockError);` and `.rejects.toThrow("already locked by this process")`; the lock file still exists; `await release()`; the file is gone; `await acquireLock(dir)` now succeeds (release it).
  - `it("lets only one of two concurrent acquisitions win")`: `const results = await Promise.allSettled([acquireLock(dir), acquireLock(dir)]);` exactly one `fulfilled`, one `rejected` with a `LockError`; release the winner; file gone.
  - `it("an old release never removes a newer holder's lock")`: `const r1 = await acquireLock(dir); await r1(); const r2 = await acquireLock(dir); await r1();` → `existsSync(path.join(dir, LOCK_FILE))` is still `true`; `await r2()` → `false`.
  - `it("release leaves a lock file it no longer owns")`: acquire, overwrite the file with `${process.pid}\nsomeone-else\n`, call release → the file still exists (content unchanged).
  - `it("takes over a lock left by a dead process or by an earlier process with this PID")`: write `${deadPid}\n` (get a dead PID by spawning `node -e "process.stdout.write(String(process.pid))"` with `execa` and awaiting it, as in `src/state/resume.test.ts:49`), acquire succeeds and the file's first line is `String(process.pid)`; release; then write `${process.pid}\n` (old format, not held in-process) and acquire again succeeds.
  - `it("refuses a lock held by another live process, in old and new format")`: write `${process.ppid}\n` then `${process.ppid}\nabc\n`; each time `acquireLock(dir)` rejects with `LockError` and message `is locked by the running process ${process.ppid}`, and `await lockHolder(dir)` returns `process.ppid` (the vitest parent process is alive and is not this process).
- **PATTERN**: `src/state/state.test.ts:7–13` (temp `dir` per test); `src/state/resume.test.ts:49` for the dead PID (`import { execa } from "execa";`).
- **GOTCHA**: always release acquired locks inside the test, or the module-level registry keeps them for the rest of the file (each test uses a fresh `dir`, so it won't fail other tests, but keep it clean).
- **VALIDATE**: `npx vitest run src/state/state.test.ts`

### VERIFY callers

- **IMPLEMENT**: no code change expected. Run the tests of every lock user: run/resume (`src/state/resume.test.ts`, `src/runner/run-retry.test.ts`, `src/state/resume-kill.test.ts`), suite (`src/suite/orchestrator.test.ts`), cleanup (`src/cleanup/scan.test.ts`), preflight (`src/launch/preflight.test.ts`). If one fails because it acquired a lock twice in one process on purpose, fix the test only when the double acquisition was the bug; otherwise re-check your change.
- **VALIDATE**: `npx vitest run src/state src/runner/run-retry.test.ts src/suite/orchestrator.test.ts src/cleanup src/launch/preflight.test.ts`

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `await acquireLock(d); await acquireLock(d)` in one process: the second call rejects with `LockError` ("run <id> is already locked by this process; release it first"); the first holder's lock file is untouched.
- [ ] Two concurrent `acquireLock(d)` calls: exactly one resolves.
- [ ] A release removes the lock file only if it still holds that acquisition's token; calling it twice, or after another acquisition took the lock, does not remove the other holder's file.
- [ ] Lock file format is `<pid>\n<token>\n`; `lockHolder` and the stale check read the first line, so old `<pid>\n` files still work (live other PID → refused, dead PID or this PID not held in-process → taken over).
- [ ] `run`, `resume`, suite start/resume, `clean` and `assertNotRunning` behave as before (their tests pass unchanged).
- [ ] `npx vitest run src/state src/runner/run-retry.test.ts src/suite/orchestrator.test.ts src/cleanup src/launch/preflight.test.ts` passes.

